  <h1>India’s Public Sector Banks — Reforms, Performance, and Future Path</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Public Sector Banks (PSBs) are the backbone of India’s financial system, holding over 60% of total banking assets. In 2024, Economic Times analysed the performance of PSBs post-recapitalisation, mergers, and governance reforms. For UPSC, this issue is central to economy, governance, and financial inclusion.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Number of PSBs: Reduced from 27 (2017) to 12 (2024) via mergers.<br/>•	Profitability: PSBs recorded ₹1.4 lakh crore profit in FY24 (record high).<br/>•	NPA Levels: Declined to ~4% (gross) from double digits in 2018.<br/>•	Credit Growth: ~12% in 2023–24, driven by retail and MSMEs.<br/>•	Financial Inclusion: PSBs key to Jan Dhan (50+ crore accounts) and DBT.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Reforms<br/>1.	Bank Consolidation: Mergers for scale and efficiency.<br/>2.	Recapitalisation: ₹3.1 lakh crore infused since 2017.<br/>3.	Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC, 2016): Better recovery of bad loans.<br/>4.	Governance Reforms: Professional boards, performance-linked incentives.<br/>5.	Digitalisation: UPI, mobile banking, AI-driven credit monitoring.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Profit Turnaround: Strong balance sheets after years of losses.<br/>•	NPA Reduction: Improved provisioning and IBC recoveries.<br/>•	Digital Leadership: PSBs like SBI leading in fintech adoption.<br/>•	Financial Inclusion: Deep rural penetration unmatched by private banks.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Governance Deficit: Political interference in lending persists.<br/>2.	Credit Appraisal: Still weaker than private counterparts.<br/>3.	Technology Gap: Slow adoption compared to fintech disruptors.<br/>4.	Capital Needs: Basel III compliance requires further capitalisation.<br/>5.	Customer Service: Long-standing inefficiencies in grievance redress.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: PSBs fuel infrastructure, MSME, and agriculture lending.<br/>•	Social Equity: Crucial for inclusive banking in rural/poor regions.<br/>•	Financial Stability: Systemic backbone during crises (COVID, IL&FS).<br/>•	Democratic Role: PSBs implement welfare-linked financial schemes.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Banking reforms, NPAs, IBC.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): State role in economy, accountability.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“India’s financial future depends on its banking backbone.”<br/>o	“Public sector banks: instruments of inclusion or inefficiency?”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: State capacity and economic governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Strengthen Governance: Independent boards, merit-based appointments.<br/>2.	Reduce Political Interference: Transparent lending practices.<br/>3.	Digital Transformation: Compete with fintech through innovation.<br/>4.	Capital Planning: Raise funds via markets, not just state recap.<br/>5.	Customer-Centric Reforms: Service quality and grievance redress focus.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s PSBs have moved from crisis to recovery, but sustained reforms are vital for long-term efficiency. Balancing their developmental mandate with competitiveness will define their future role. For UPSC aspirants, PSBs highlight the political economy of banking in India.</p>
